PROJECT REPORT

Title: Personalized Water Intake Tracker


1. Problem Statement


In daily life, most people either underestimate or overestimate how much water they should drink. The common “8 glasses a day” rule is too generic and ignores important personal factors such as body weight, age, lifestyle, and health conditions.
This leads to poor hydration habits, which can affect energy levels, concentration, digestion, and overall health.
The problem I chose to address was:
How can we provide a simple, personalized way for individuals to understand their daily water intake needs?


2. Why This Problem Matters


Water intake is a basic yet often neglected aspect of health. While people track calories, workouts, and sleep, hydration is usually overlooked or handled casually.
The issue becomes more important because:
Hydration needs vary from person to person
Health conditions can change water requirements
Lack of awareness leads to inconsistent habits
A simple and accessible solution can help users make better daily decisions without needing complex medical knowledge.


3. Approach to the Solution


I decided to build a web-based tool using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript that calculates recommended daily water intake based on user input.
The approach included:
Taking user inputs:
Age
Weight
Height
Gender
Health conditions
Applying a basic scientific formula (water intake per kg of body weight)
Adjusting the result based on:
Age (slightly reduced needs for older individuals)
Gender differences
Height (larger body size factor)
Health conditions (modifications for safety)
Presenting the output in a clear and conversational way, rather than just showing numbers
The goal was not just calculation, but making the result understandable and relatable.


4. Key Decisions


During development, a few important decisions shaped the project:
a. Simplicity over complexity
Instead of building a complicated system, I focused on a clean and usable interface that anyone can understand.
b. Human-centered output
Rather than displaying only numbers, I added short explanations so users understand why a certain amount is suggested.
c. No external frameworks
The project uses only basic HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, making it lightweight and easy to run.
d. Health-sensitive adjustments
Special conditions like kidney or heart issues were handled carefully by reducing or modifying recommendations and adding advisory notes.


5. Challenges Faced


a. Balancing accuracy and simplicity
It was challenging to keep the calculation scientifically reasonable while still keeping the logic simple enough to implement.
b. Handling health conditions
Different medical conditions require precise advice, but since this is not a medical tool, I had to ensure the output remained safe and general.
c. Making the UI feel natural
Avoiding a robotic interface and making it feel more like a helpful guide required careful wording and layout.


6. What I Learned


This project helped me understand:
How to translate a real-world problem into a functional web solution
The importance of user experience, not just functionality
Basic logic building in JavaScript
How small design choices can make a tool feel more intuitive
The value of keeping things simple but meaningful


7. Conclusion


The water intake tracker provides a practical and personalized way for users to understand their hydration needs. While it is not a medical tool, it serves as a helpful guide for building better daily habits.
The project demonstrates how even a simple application can make everyday decisions clearer and more informed.
